1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,000
Το Plex ζήτησε 120€ για να συνεχίσετε να βλέπετε από τις συσκευές σας.
Το πλήρωσα εγώ το ποσό.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,000
Αν χρησιμοποιείτε συχνά και μπορείτε,
 οποιαδήποτε συμβολή θα εκτιμηθεί.

3
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:38,760
Long-range magic is a combination of
three key factors essential to mages.

4
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:41,260
There's the amount of mana,

5
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:45,350
the strength with which it's fired, and the
level of control the mage has over it.

6
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:47,350
You know that much, don't you?

7
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:48,140
Yes.

8
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:51,940
Earlier, your magic dissipated
before it reached its target.

9
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:55,780
That's a sign that your mana
and firing strength are lacking.

10
00:01:56,190 --> 00:01:59,410
It's not something you can fix overnight.

11
00:01:59,410 --> 00:02:03,530
Regardless of your talent,
it'll take years to master.

12
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:13,250
However, your level of control
over the magic is very high.

13
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:15,630
That's what most people
tend to have trouble with.

14
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:18,130
But you've got no problems there,

15
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:20,630
so just be patient with yourself.

16
00:02:22,930 --> 00:02:23,970
Okay.

17
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:36,840
Is Fern's training coming along smoothly?

18
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:41,170
In just four years, she's learned more
than most people do in ten.

19
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:44,800
She's studying too hard.
It's not a good thing.

20
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,350
She's been spending all her
time in the woods lately.

21
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:51,060
She must really love magic.

22
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:54,980
But she's still a long way
from becoming a proper mage.

23
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:58,270
I'll finish deciphering the
grimoire before she does.

24
00:03:59,190 --> 00:04:00,610
I see.

25
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:03,990
Say, Heiter.

26
00:04:04,610 --> 00:04:06,950
This grimoire probably...

27
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,250
Heiter?

28
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:21,460
Don't look at me like that.

29
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:25,930
If anything, it's a miracle
I've been mobile until now.

30
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,350
I'll try to decipher the grimoire quickly.

31
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:34,350
I'd appreciate that.

32
00:04:39,570 --> 00:04:42,110
Fern, put your training on hold.

33
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:45,240
Heiter collapsed.
I need you to stay with him.

34
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:49,070
But I still haven't hit the rock.

35
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:53,200
You'll do it eventually. Right now—

36
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:54,830
Eventually isn't good enough.

37
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:01,590
Mr. Heiter will die before then.

38
00:05:04,210 --> 00:05:07,090
He saved my life.

39
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:21,520
It'd be a waste for you to die now.

40
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,070
A waste?

41
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:31,490
A long time ago, I lost an old friend.

42
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:35,200
Unlike me, he was straight as an arrow,

43
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:39,330
and he never abandoned
anyone who was in need.

44
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:46,510
If he'd survived instead of me,
he would've saved a lot of people.

45
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:52,970
Since I'm not like him, I planned to
live out the rest of my days quietly,

46
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:56,520
but one day, I suddenly realized something.

47
00:05:57,390 --> 00:05:59,770
If I died without doing anything,

48
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:03,480
all the courage, strength of will,
and friendship I learned from him—

49
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:09,150
even all my precious memories—
would vanish from this world.

50
00:06:12,490 --> 00:06:16,910
If you have precious memories
inside yourself as well,

51
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:20,500
then I think it'd be a waste for you to die.

52
00:06:22,670 --> 00:06:27,130
Mr. Heiter has always feared
dying and leaving me alone.

53
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:31,010
He did the right thing.

54
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:34,260
I don't want him to regret saving me.

55
00:06:34,890 --> 00:06:37,270
I don't care if it's as a mage
or whatever else—

56
00:06:37,270 --> 00:06:42,020
learning some kind of skill to support
myself is my way of repaying him.

57
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:47,400
I want him to be glad he saved me
and to know I'll be fine.

58
00:06:51,030 --> 00:06:54,280
You remember everything
I've taught you, don't you?

59
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:55,120
Yes.

60
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:57,540
Then do as you like.

61
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:02,140
It didn't contain any spells for bringing
back the dead or granting immortality.

62
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:03,770
I see.

63
00:08:04,190 --> 00:08:05,270
Did you know that already?

64
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:09,400
The fear of death is immeasurable.

65
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:14,150
If such spells existed,
Ewig would've cast them on himself.

66
00:08:14,150 --> 00:08:15,780
Then why?

67
00:08:17,030 --> 00:08:19,330
How is Fern?

68
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:21,870
She has room for improvement in some areas,

69
00:08:22,370 --> 00:08:25,540
but it wouldn't be wrong
to call her a proper mage.

70
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,210
I see. Then she made it in time.

71
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:35,010
Now she won't get in your way, Frieren.

72
00:08:37,010 --> 00:08:40,220
Will you bring her with you on your travels?

73
00:08:40,810 --> 00:08:43,520
I'm sorry, Heiter.
That's the one thing I can't do.

74
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:46,270
She'd only get in my way.

75
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:48,980
You tricked me, Heiter.

76
00:08:52,070 --> 00:08:55,320
Your payment for deciphering
the grimoire is in the desk drawer.

77
00:08:56,110 --> 00:08:58,820
Please leave this place tonight.

78
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:00,780
Why?

79
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:05,410
As you can see, I don't have much longer.

80
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:12,800
I don't want her to experience
losing anyone else.

81
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:17,800
Frieren, please look after Fern.

82
00:09:19,850 --> 00:09:22,810
Are you trying to put on
a brave face again, Heiter?

83
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:28,730
Fern has been ready to say goodbye
to you for some time now.

84
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:32,270
What you need to do before your death

85
00:09:32,270 --> 00:09:35,070
is give her a proper farewell

86
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:38,700
and make as many memories as you can.

87
00:09:41,830 --> 00:09:47,080
Frieren, you really are a kind girl.

88
00:09:56,420 --> 00:09:59,720
Why did you save Fern?

89
00:10:16,740 --> 00:10:19,740
It's what Himmel the Hero would've done.

90
00:10:22,450 --> 00:10:23,620
You're right.

91
00:10:37,130 --> 00:10:41,470
Then maybe I'll do the same.

92
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:53,310
Thank you.

93
00:10:53,730 --> 00:10:57,820
Because of you, I was able to
repay my debt to Mr. Heiter.

94
00:10:59,190 --> 00:11:01,860
He just tricked me, is all.

95
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:05,240
This corrupt priest.

96
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:12,250
Shall we go, then?

97
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,110
That's enough, Fern.

98
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:37,520
We're finished.

99
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:43,360
Thank you for your help.

100
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,950
Here's your reward.

101
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:50,330
We've been doing such simple work.

102
00:11:50,330 --> 00:11:52,370
That's how adventuring goes.

103
00:11:52,710 --> 00:11:54,920
What were we paid in?

104
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:58,880
Folk magic. It's a spell
that produces warm tea.

105
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:00,340
We'll try it out later.

106
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:05,470
The other day we learned a spell
for removing rust from bronze statues.

107
00:12:06,050 --> 00:12:10,430
Before that, we learned a spell
for turning sweet grapes into sour ones.

108
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:09,260
How is it?

109
00:12:09,260 --> 00:12:10,350
Sour.

110
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:13,770
You keep collecting the strangest spells.

111
00:12:13,770 --> 00:12:15,060
It's my hobby, after all.

112
00:12:15,650 --> 00:12:19,570
Ms. Frieren, you really do love magic.

113
00:12:19,570 --> 00:12:22,320
Only somewhat, like you.

114
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:25,570
I feel like we're a little different.

115
00:12:26,910 --> 00:12:28,160
We're the same.

116
00:12:29,530 --> 00:12:33,160
Will you really do something for me?

117
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:38,460
Since I'm an herbalist,
I don't have any spells to teach you.

118
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:43,840
That's fine. Just learning about
the local flora is helpful enough.

119
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:49,470
This is it.

120
00:12:54,850 --> 00:12:57,400
It's a statue of Himmel the Hero.

121
00:12:57,730 --> 00:12:59,860
It's in terrible shape, isn't it?

122
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:03,610
It's too much for one old woman to handle.

123
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:07,280
And the villagers don't care anymore.

124
00:13:10,030 --> 00:13:11,120
Run!

125
00:13:30,430 --> 00:13:34,140
When the village was attacked by a monster,

126
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:36,600
Mr. Himmel fought valiantly to defend us.

127
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:39,940
It saddens me to see him treated like this.

128
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:46,900
No, it's what he deserves.
It's Himmel's fault for wanting to show off.

129
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:51,830
When the villagers offered to make a statue
of him, he should have just declined.

130
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:54,790
Is this okay?

131
00:13:54,790 --> 00:13:56,790
This pose isn't too handsome, is it?

132
00:13:56,790 --> 00:13:59,040
Just hurry up and pick something.

133
00:13:57,370 --> 00:14:01,540
He drove the sculptor mad
by spending 18 hours choosing a pose.

134
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:00,460
I'm hungry.

135
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:06,510
In the end, they settled on
a ridiculously plain one.

136
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:09,430
What a strange story.

137
00:14:10,010 --> 00:14:14,930
Come to think of it, there was an
elven mage in the hero's party.

138
00:14:16,730 --> 00:14:18,560
Shall we get this cleaned up?

139
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:24,320
Thank you.

140
00:14:24,730 --> 00:14:27,110
Magic sure is amazing, isn't it?

141
00:14:27,110 --> 00:14:29,570
There isn't a speck of rust left.

142
00:14:32,070 --> 00:14:34,580
I wish there was a bit more color, though.

143
00:14:34,580 --> 00:14:36,950
Perhaps I'll plant some flowers later.

144
00:14:37,450 --> 00:14:41,540
Ms. Frieren, you have a spell that
produces a field of flowers, right?

145
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:44,920
You're right. I guess I could just
conjure something up...

146
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:51,380
No, blue moon weed flowers would be best.

147
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:54,050
What kind of flowers are those?

148
00:14:54,050 --> 00:14:56,430
I don't know. I've never seen them.

149
00:14:56,430 --> 00:14:57,560
Then why?

150
00:14:58,220 --> 00:15:00,190
They grow in Himmel's hometown.

151
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:03,400
But you can't conjure flowers
you've never seen before.

152
00:15:10,280 --> 00:15:11,820
Blue moon weed...

153
00:15:12,110 --> 00:15:14,280
Now that's a name I haven't
heard in a long time.

154
00:15:16,620 --> 00:15:21,830
Long ago, there used to be a
field of them in those woods.

155
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:29,710
They don't grow around here anymore?

156
00:15:30,550 --> 00:15:32,590
They all died out.

157
00:15:33,050 --> 00:15:37,220
It's been decades since they've
been spotted in these lands.

158
00:15:38,180 --> 00:15:39,270
I see.

159
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:44,850
Let's go, Fern.

160
00:15:44,850 --> 00:15:45,860
Okay.

161
00:15:50,190 --> 00:15:52,070
You're hiding something.

162
00:15:52,650 --> 00:15:54,700
I won't be mad, so show me what it is.

163
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:02,620
They're seed rats. Pests that consume seeds.

164
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:04,330
I'll release them in the woods.

165
00:16:08,880 --> 00:16:12,090
Fern, shall we go look for blue moon weed?

166
00:16:12,510 --> 00:16:14,220
Are you serious?

167
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:17,760
There was a field of them
in these woods until recently.

168
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:19,060
That was decades ago.

169
00:16:19,470 --> 00:16:21,310
But they're worth looking for.

170
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:28,150
If we find and analyze them, we can acquire
a spell for conjuring blue moon weed flowers.

171
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:30,650
For Mr. Himmel?

172
00:16:31,280 --> 00:16:32,150
No...

173
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:34,820
I'm probably doing this for myself.

174
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:33,380
Ms. Frieren, we've been searching for
blue moon weed for six months now.

175
00:17:33,710 --> 00:17:37,630
You're right. Perhaps we should
broaden our search area.

176
00:17:46,730 --> 00:17:50,520
Oh, I don't often see you on your own.

177
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:56,740
It's been a while since you
first came to the village.

178
00:17:57,950 --> 00:18:00,660
Have you found any blue moon weed?

179
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,240
Do you think we'll find any?

180
00:18:05,290 --> 00:18:08,790
Ms. Frieren's obsession with
magic is extraordinary.

181
00:18:09,370 --> 00:18:13,750
At this rate, she'll continue searching
for years, if not decades.

182
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:19,220
She's a mage who possesses the
power to save many people.

183
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:25,390
She shouldn't spend her time searching
for something that doesn't exist.

184
00:18:26,980 --> 00:18:28,560
You're so young.

185
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:33,230
Am I wrong?

186
00:18:33,900 --> 00:18:35,900
I don't think so,

187
00:18:37,150 --> 00:18:41,870
but Ms. Frieren must see things differently.

188
00:18:44,620 --> 00:18:49,580
But since she's so much more
mature than we are,

189
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:54,040
I'm sure she'd understand if you honestly
conveyed those feelings to her.

190
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:16,900
These are the seeds of a related species
she kept for their medicinal properties.

191
00:19:17,650 --> 00:19:21,490
Could we plant these around
Himmel's statue instead?

192
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:23,910
I get it, Fern.

193
00:19:24,450 --> 00:19:26,450
I made you worry.

194
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:32,250
I'm not just spending my own time on this.

195
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:34,040
It's time to go.

196
00:19:34,710 --> 00:19:37,050
I'll end my search soon.

197
00:19:39,510 --> 00:19:42,180
How many years is soon?

198
00:19:42,550 --> 00:19:43,970
Soon.

199
00:19:44,550 --> 00:19:47,510
You don't know when to stop—

200
00:19:59,110 --> 00:20:00,740
Shall we follow it?

201
00:20:30,890 --> 00:20:34,770
Ms. Frieren, why do you collect spells?

202
00:20:35,190 --> 00:20:36,440
It's just a hobby of mine.

203
00:20:36,730 --> 00:20:38,440
I find that hard to believe.

204
00:20:39,190 --> 00:20:41,440
It really is just a hobby.

205
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:45,820
I used to live much more apathetically.

206
00:21:11,260 --> 00:21:12,470
Gross.

207
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:24,860
But then I got to know an idiot who
complimented the spells I gathered.

208
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:26,650
That's all.

209
00:21:28,660 --> 00:21:30,450
That's a silly reason.

210
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:32,370
You're right.

211
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:55,600
Is that...

212
00:21:55,930 --> 00:21:57,390
It's a petal.

213
00:21:58,060 --> 00:22:03,360
They say seed rats bury their food
in safe places away from enemies.

214
00:22:03,900 --> 00:22:05,940
They're clever animals, aren't they?

215
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:07,780
I wouldn't say that.

216
00:22:08,650 --> 00:22:13,200
Since they bury their food in so many places,
they often forget where.

217
00:22:17,790 --> 00:22:18,960
Blue moon weed?

218
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:23,460
It's a flower that grows in
my homeland. It's beautiful.

219
00:22:24,420 --> 00:22:26,880
Not as beautiful as me, though.

220
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:28,470
Shall we get going?

221
00:22:29,880 --> 00:22:30,470
Frieren.

222
00:22:31,720 --> 00:22:33,680
Someday, I'd like to show them to you.

223
00:22:35,010 --> 00:22:35,720
I see.

224
00:22:36,100 --> 00:22:37,680
If we have the opportunity, perhaps.

225
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:49,780
I figured they'd be here,
but I wasn't expecting so many.

226
00:22:54,120 --> 00:22:56,870
<i>It took longer than I would've liked, Himmel.</i>

227
00:23:02,420 --> 00:23:05,000
I can't believe we really found them.

228
00:23:05,380 --> 00:23:08,210
Now we can create a spell for blue moon weed.

229
00:23:08,840 --> 00:23:11,680
Why are you so passionate about magic?

230
00:23:12,220 --> 00:23:13,760
I don't understand.

231
00:23:14,510 --> 00:23:16,010
But you do.

232
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:20,020
You never gave up on becoming a mage.

233
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:23,150
That's different.

234
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:28,230
I would've been happy to learn any skill
that allowed me to support myself.

235
00:23:29,240 --> 00:23:31,400
It didn't have to be magic...

236
00:23:32,030 --> 00:23:35,280
But you chose magic.

237
00:23:47,630 --> 00:23:48,920
You're right.

238
00:23:50,970 --> 00:23:55,760
I never thought I'd see blue moon weed again.

239
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:57,890
They're beautiful.

240
00:23:59,060 --> 00:24:01,140
Thank you, Ms. Frieren.

241
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:05,400
Now this statue will never be forgotten.

242
00:24:05,770 --> 00:24:08,940
Oh, I nearly forgot.

243
00:24:10,280 --> 00:24:11,240
There.

244
00:24:12,360 --> 00:24:15,160
How cute.

245
00:24:15,780 --> 00:24:17,410
Shall we go?

246
00:25:50,290 --> 00:25:52,630
That's too powerful.

247
00:25:51,420 --> 00:25:53,420
The Elder Sage of Corruption, Qual.

248
00:25:52,590 --> 00:25:55,800
He called you heartless for not checking on him.

249
00:25:53,630 --> 00:25:55,800
Let's put that into practice.

250
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:56,800
How many years do you think
we've traveled together?

251
00:25:55,800 --> 00:25:57,970
Does this journey have a purpose?

252
00:25:55,800 --> 00:25:57,970
Classroom study is important, too.

253
00:25:57,970 --> 00:26:00,050
Killing magic.
